try to make sure that
our customer understands

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:53.085
our product and how it is
differentiated in the market.

00:03:53.085 --> 00:03:53.960
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:03:53.960 --> 00:03:55.168
So I have to admit something.

00:03:55.168 --> 00:03:58.790
I haven't touched a relational
database in a really long time,

00:03:58.790 --> 00:04:01.220
just because I do weird
things, mostly in game worlds.

00:04:01.220 --> 00:04:02.490
But a lot of people do.

00:04:02.490 --> 00:04:06.490
So can you give us, like, at a
high level, what is Cloud SQL?

00:04:06.490 --> 00:04:08.960
AMY KRISHNAMOHAN: So Cloud
SQL, in the very simple term,

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:12.380
manages services for
open source databases,

00:04:12.380 --> 00:04:14.720
like MySQL and Postgres.

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:17.329
So relational database
has been existent

00:04:17.329 --> 00:04:19.339
for almost 30, 40 years.

00:04:19.339 --> 00:04:21.740
So I think everybody
kind of knows a lot what

00:04:21.740 --> 00:04:23.420
relational database is.

00:04:23.420 --> 00:04:27.860
But nowadays, you've seen
the emergence of databases

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:28.880
everywhere.

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:31.720
I think in the DV
engine ranking itself,

00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:35.210
there is 250 databases
existing right now.

00:04:35.210 --> 00:04:38.600
But interestingly, still,
the relational databases,

00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:41.720
like MySQL and Postgres,
is still dominant database

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:43.190
that everyone is using.

00:04:43.190 --> 00:04:45.740
So we are providing a
fully managed service

00:04:45.740 --> 00:04:48.260
for those open source
databases so that people

00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:50.900
can use those database
without really having

00:04:50.900 --> 00:04:52.225
a burden of managing them.

00:04:52.225 --> 00:04:53.600
GABRIELA FERRARA:
Thank you, Amy.

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:56.900
So beyond Cloud SQL,
what other products

00:04:56.900 --> 00:04:59.543
do we support on Google
Cloud for the databases?

00:04:59.543 --> 00:05:00.460
AMY KRISHNAMOHAN: Yes.

00:05:00.460 --> 00:05:02.940
So Google Cloud
database actually offer

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:06.080
a wide variety of databases
to serve various needs

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:07.520
that customers have.

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:10.400
So Cloud SQL is focusing
on relational database

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:13.940
for open source database,
like MySQL and Postgres.

00:05:13.940 --> 00:05:17.020
And then we also have
a Cloud Memorystore,

00:05:17.020 --> 00:05:20.890
which provides a fully managed
service for Redis, free memory

00:05:20.890 --> 00:05:21.520
caching.

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:24.250
And then there are also
other interesting databases

00:05:24.250 --> 00:05:26.040
are born at Google.

00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:28.990
The one is Cloud
Bigtable-- so we call it

00:05:28.990 --> 00:05:33.250
as a NoSQL wide column
database services, which powers

00:05:33.250 --> 00:05:35.830
a lot of service like YouTube.

00:05:35.830 --> 00:05:39.980
And then the Cloud Firestore,
which is document databases.

00:05:39.980 --> 00:05:43.690
And it stores a lot of
mobile and web app data.

00:05:43.690 --> 00:05:44.740
And it is serverless.

00:05:44.740 --> 00:05:48.010
It is very integrated
with Firebase,

00:05:48.010 --> 00:05:51.520
so it is extremely easy to
build a mobile application based

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:52.550
on that.

00:05:52.550 --> 00:05:56.080
And finally, I think the most
shining star database at Google

00:05:56.080 --> 00:05:57.115
is Cloud Spanner.

00:05:57.115 --> 00:05:58.300
[NOTIFICATION SOUNDS]

00:05:58.300 --> 00:06:02.660
It is a relational database
and globally consistent.

00:06:02.660 --> 00:06:04.810
Which is, actually--
I think everybody

00:06:04.810 --> 00:06:06.500
thought that it was impossible.

00:06:06.500 --> 00:06:08.440
So it's something
that Google made

00:06:08.440 --> 00:06:09.690
impossible possible databases.

00:06:09.690 --> 00:06:11.470
GABRIELA FERRARA: Yes. it's
that [INAUDIBLE],, right?

00:06:11.470 --> 00:06:11.770
AMY KRISHNAMOHAN: Mhm.

00:06:11.770 --> 00:06:12.070
Yes.

00:06:12.070 --> 00:06:12.880
MARK MANDEL: Yes.

00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:15.172
And if people want to hear
about Spanner Episode Number

00:06:15.172 --> 00:06:17.410
62 of the Google Cloud
Platform podcast.

00:06:17.410 --> 00:06:19.900
I actually would love
to hear just your take

00:06:19.900 --> 00:06:21.300
on relational data storage.

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:22.820
I expect there's probably
some of our listeners

00:06:22.820 --> 00:06:25.510
who probably grew up maybe just
working on something like Mongo

00:06:25.510 --> 00:06:27.583
or are working on
something unstructured.

00:06:27.583 --> 00:06:30.250
So I'd love to hear your take on
what is relational data storage

00:06:30.250 --> 00:06:32.992
and maybe how has it changed
in the last 30 odd years?

00:06:32.992 --> 00:06:35.200
AMY KRISHNAMOHAN: So relational
databases is really--

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:37.940
the easiest way to
think is, like, table.

00:06:37.940 --> 00:06:41.620
So if you look at the sheet,
you have a really clean table

00:06:41.620 --> 00:06:44.440
that has a defined
column, defined row,

00:06:44.440 --> 00:06:47.140
and the data is
extremely ordered.

00:06:47.140 --> 00:06:49.750
So before you
store the data, you

00:06:49.750 --> 00:06:51.820
need to know what
data type it is.

00:06:51.820 --> 00:06:53.190
Is it going to be number?

00:06:53.190 --> 00:06:56.170
Is it going to be text, what
the size is going to be.

00:06:56.170 --> 00:06:58.730
So it all needs
to be predefined.

00:06:58.730 --> 00:07:01.480
So I would call that
relational databases really,

00:07:01.480 --> 00:07:05.340
really structured and
well-managed databases.

00:07:05.340 --> 00:07:08.650
So it is mainly used for a
lot of a transactional system,

00:07:08.650 --> 00:07:13.060
like financial system or ERP
systems, where you know exactly

00:07:13.060 --> 00:07:15.080
what data is going to come in.

00:07:15.080 --> 00:07:18.250
So a lot of people these
days are using NoSQL

00:07:18.250 --> 00:07:19.900
or unstructured databases.

00:07:19.900 --> 00:07:23.080
Those are when you don't know
the data, what kind of data

00:07:23.080 --> 00:07:23.800
is coming in.

00:07:23.800 --> 00:07:27.020
You can just sort the data
first and then analyze later.

00:07:27.020 --> 00:07:30.370
So there are pros and
cons for each databases.

00:07:30.370 --> 00:07:33.760
Because relational database
is extremely well-structured,

00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:36.070
it is actually much
easier to analyze data.

00:07:36.070 --> 00:07:39.640
Because you know exactly
what is in it and then

00:07:39.640 --> 00:07:41.770
you can use a SQL
language, which

00:07:41.770 --> 00:07:45.620
is actually very easy to learn,
and query those data easily.

00:07:45.620 --> 00:07:49.180
Whereas if you're using NoSQL,
or the unstructured database,

00:07:49.180 --> 00:07:51.460
like Hadoop, you
really don't know what

00:07:51.460 --> 00:07:53.650
data is inside your databases.

00:07:53.650 --> 00:07:56.320
So you need to actually
identify and investigate

00:07:56.320 --> 00:07:58.770
what data you have
and also build

00:07:58.770 --> 00:08:01.720
a kind of custom analytics
to analyze those data.

00:08:01.720 --> 00:08:03.640
It has very different use cases.

00:08:03.640 --> 00:08:05.600
It is sometimes a
relational database

00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:07.870
are just [INAUDIBLE]
for certain use cases.

00:08:07.870 --> 00:08:10.300
And then unstructured,
NoSQL databases,

00:08:10.300 --> 00:08:12.430
is good for certain use cases.

00:08:12.430 --> 00:08:14.060
MARK MANDEL: What
sort of use cases?

00:08:14.060 --> 00:08:15.852
AMY KRISHNAMOHAN: So
a lot of the use cases

00:08:15.852 --> 00:08:20.170
that we see like IoT, where
data are just pouring in,

00:08:20.170 --> 00:08:24.170
or we have a lot of
social media, or gaming,

00:08:24.170 --> 00:08:27.310
they're use cases that our
data is not really structured.

00:08:27.310 --> 00:08:30.730
Those are really good for
the Hadoop, NoSQL databases.

00:08:30.730 --> 00:08:34.330
And then if the data is
actually extremely important,

00:08:34.330 --> 00:08:39.093
like transaction-wise, and then
you cannot lose those data.

00:08:39.093 --> 00:08:40.510
For example, if
you are depositing

00:08:40.510 --> 00:08:44.169
the money in the bank, you
need that data to be consistent

00:08:44.169 --> 00:08:47.620
and stored safely in
certain databases.

00:08:47.620 --> 00:08:50.620
So you cannot have, like on
social media, NoSQL database.

00:08:50.620 --> 00:08:53.750
So you have to put those data
in the relational databases.

00:08:53.750 --> 00:08:55.700
GABRIELA FERRARA: So
how does Cloud SQL

00:08:55.700 --> 00:08:58.558
scale inside the
Google Cloud platform?

00:08:58.558 --> 00:09:00.100
AMY KRISHNAMOHAN:
So Cloud SQL scales

00:09:00.100 --> 00:09:01.183
in the two different ways.

00:09:01.183 --> 00:09:05.590
First one's a scale out, and
the other one's a scale up.

00:09:05.590 --> 00:09:08.650
So scale out is
using read replicas.

00:09:08.650 --> 00:09:11.330
When there is a lot
of tactics coming in,

00:09:11.330 --> 00:09:13.780
there's a replica
of the Cloud SQL.

00:09:13.780 --> 00:09:15.880
You can create it
in different zone.

00:09:15.880 --> 00:09:18.430
And you can route those
traffic to the read replica

00:09:18.430 --> 00:09:21.520
so that not one
instance doesn't get

00:09:21.520 --> 00:09:23.690
bombarded with so many traffic.

00:09:23.690 --> 00:09:26.340
So all the traffic can be
routed different, and so on.

00:09:26.340 --> 00:09:29.440
And then the other
one, the scaling up,

00:09:29.440 --> 00:09:31.570
where we can just
scale the storage,

00:09:31.570 --> 00:09:34.430
we have automatic
storage increase feature.

00:09:34.430 --> 00:09:36.610
So whenever data is
coming in, our storage

00:09:36.610 --> 00:09:39.190
will increase the size
automatically for you.

00:09:39.190 --> 00:09:42.023
MARK MANDEL: Can you also scale
up in terms of memory and CPU?

00:09:42.023 --> 00:09:42.940
Or is it just storage?

00:09:42.940 --> 00:09:44.170
AMY KRISHNAMOHAN:
Just the storage.

00:09:44.170 --> 00:09:45.670
GABBY FERRARA: Which
is interesting,

00:09:45.670 --> 00:09:49.340
because if you're importing
files, like a lot of stuff,

00:09:49.340 --> 00:09:52.360
and then, whoa,
your disc is full.

00:09:52.360 --> 00:09:53.250
It's like, yeah.

00:09:53.250 --> 00:09:54.222
That's not cool.

00:09:54.222 --> 00:09:55.180
AMY KRISHNAMOHAN: Yeah.

00:09:55.180 --> 00:09:58.360
We actually have a customer
case where one of our customers

00:09:58.360 --> 00:10:01.760
tried to enter all the GIS
information in the Postgres

00:10:01.760 --> 00:10:04.090
instance at classical.

00:10:04.090 --> 00:10:09.280
And he was using on prem
initially, and after three hour

00:10:09.280 --> 00:10:10.877
the job failed.

00:10:10.877 --> 00:10:12.460
And then when the
job failed, it means

00:10:12.460 --> 00:10:14.085
that everything got
canceled and rolled

00:10:14.085 --> 00:10:17.200
back to original state, which
is actually very devastating,

00:10:17.200 --> 00:10:19.000
after waiting three hours.

00:10:19.000 --> 00:10:20.470
But when they were
using Cloud SQL,

00:10:20.470 --> 00:10:23.590
they could see that the storage
was increasing automatically

00:10:23.590 --> 00:10:26.070
as a job is progressing.

00:10:26.070 --> 00:10:27.460
MARK MANDEL: Oh, nice.

00:10:27.460 --> 00:10:29.230
Are there any
interesting trade offs

00:10:29.230 --> 00:10:32.210
between like scaling
out, scaling up

00:10:32.210 --> 00:10:34.780
that people should be aware
of, or people keep in mind

00:10:34.780 --> 00:10:36.100
while doing scaling?

00:10:36.100 --> 00:10:38.410
AMY KRISHNAMOHAN: When
you're actually scaling up,

00:10:38.410 --> 00:10:42.860
the IOPs size is actually
dependant on the storage size.

00:10:42.860 --> 00:10:46.120
So when you actually
provision your instances,

00:10:46.120 --> 00:10:50.050
we always recommend to
provision the storage

00:10:50.050 --> 00:10:53.740
in a bigger capacity if
you want to get more IOPs.

00:10:53.740 --> 00:10:57.580
So when you actually provision
Cloud SQL in our console,

00:10:57.580 --> 00:11:01.750
you will be able to see the IOPs
metrics automatically increase

00:11:01.750 --> 00:11:03.858
as you assign more storages.

00:11:03.858 --> 00:11:05.650
So that is actually a
very important point.

00:11:05.650 --> 00:11:08.200
GABBY FERRARA: So all the
features that Cloud SQL has

00:11:08.200 --> 00:11:10.750
and the features that
you talked about here,

00:11:10.750 --> 00:11:12.760
is there any one
that's especially

00:11:12.760 --> 00:11:16.120
for you that you
like, that is more

00:11:16.120 --> 00:11:17.382
particular for your liking?

00:11:17.382 --> 00:11:18.340
AMY KRISHNAMOHAN: Yeah.

00:11:18.340 --> 00:11:21.520
Actually, one of the features
that I think is very popular

00:11:21.520 --> 00:11:26.140
is a high availability feature
that we recently announced

00:11:26.140 --> 00:11:29.110
at the Postgres instances.

00:11:29.110 --> 00:11:32.470
So classical or Postgres has
a high availability feature,

00:11:32.470 --> 00:11:35.500
where we have a
primary instance,

00:11:35.500 --> 00:11:38.740
and there is a standby
instance that it is really not

00:11:38.740 --> 00:11:40.760
feasible to customers.

00:11:40.760 --> 00:11:43.210
But what the standby
instances do,

00:11:43.210 --> 00:11:46.000
they always connect with
the primary instance.

00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:49.970
And they replicate those
data asynchronously.

00:11:49.970 --> 00:11:52.240
So it doesn't really
impact the performance.

00:11:52.240 --> 00:11:55.000
It happens really transparently.

00:11:55.000 --> 00:11:57.800
So customer will
not notice anything.

00:11:57.800 --> 00:12:00.520
And then what we do is we
also do continuous health

00:12:00.520 --> 00:12:01.700
monitoring.

00:12:01.700 --> 00:12:04.240
So we keep sending a
signal to primary instance

00:12:04.240 --> 00:12:07.190
to see if the primary
instance is healthy.

00:12:07.190 --> 00:12:09.260
And if we don't
hear anything back,

00:12:09.260 --> 00:12:12.400
I think it is about 60
seconds we automatically

00:12:12.400 --> 00:12:15.970
switch primary instances
to standby instances.

00:12:15.970 --> 00:12:18.110
Failover happens automatically.

00:12:18.110 --> 00:12:22.300
So application will not notice
when the system goes down,

00:12:22.300 --> 00:12:25.270
because we have standby
instances that are fully backed

00:12:25.270 --> 00:12:28.388
up to serve that older traffic.

00:12:28.388 --> 00:12:29.680
MARK MANDEL: That's super cool.

00:12:29.680 --> 00:12:31.690
That also gives me another
interesting question

00:12:31.690 --> 00:12:34.870
around updates of
MySQL or Postgres.

00:12:34.870 --> 00:12:35.787
How are those managed?

00:12:35.787 --> 00:12:37.328
Cause obviously,
I'm guessing there's

00:12:37.328 --> 00:12:38.510
new versions that come out.

00:12:38.510 --> 00:12:40.360
And people are going
to want to upgrade.

00:12:40.360 --> 00:12:42.400
I'm guessing that's part of
the whole managed service?

00:12:42.400 --> 00:12:42.700
AMY KRISHNAMOHAN: Yes.

00:12:42.700 --> 00:12:45.730
So our managed service provides
all the updates, the version

00:12:45.730 --> 00:12:48.860
update, OS patch,
and things like that.

00:12:48.860 --> 00:12:53.620
So there is a maintenance window
where a customer can choose.

00:12:53.620 --> 00:12:56.480
And from that, we provide
all the maintenance.

00:12:56.480 --> 00:12:58.370
So there is a little
bit of downtime

00:12:58.370 --> 00:13:00.020
while that is happening.

00:13:00.020 --> 00:13:02.380
But we take care of all
the updates for you.

00:13:02.380 --> 00:13:03.970
GABBY FERRARA: What
kind of features

00:13:03.970 --> 00:13:07.655
would you like to see in
the future being implemented

00:13:07.655 --> 00:13:09.220
on managed databases.

00:13:09.220 --> 00:13:12.670
Not necessarily Cloud SQL, but
managed databases in general,

00:13:12.670 --> 00:13:17.320
that you see normal like on prem
have, but on managed databases,

00:13:17.320 --> 00:13:18.670
you don't have yet.

00:13:18.670 --> 00:13:20.878
So what kind of features
would you like to see there?

00:13:20.878 --> 00:13:23.087
AMY KRISHNAMOHAN: I think
that the most important one

00:13:23.087 --> 00:13:25.150
is really having an
integrated experience.

00:13:25.150 --> 00:13:29.100
So like streaming data to
the data warehousing system.

00:13:29.100 --> 00:13:32.350
So all the transactional data
can go to an analytics platform

00:13:32.350 --> 00:13:34.600
very easily, and back and forth.

00:13:34.600 --> 00:13:37.330
Or have some sort
of federated query,

00:13:37.330 --> 00:13:41.080
where you can query the data
from transaction system, as

00:13:41.080 --> 00:13:44.440
well as analytic
system back and forth,

00:13:44.440 --> 00:13:47.800
so that people don't
have image about like,

00:13:47.800 --> 00:13:51.040
hey, I need to go to this
database to get this data,

00:13:51.040 --> 00:13:53.380
or go to the other database
to get this data, having

00:13:53.380 --> 00:13:55.030
this kind of data silo.

00:13:55.030 --> 00:13:58.780
So really having
universal data platform

00:13:58.780 --> 00:14:01.017
access, that will be actually
a really great feature.

00:14:01.017 --> 00:14:02.850
MARK MANDEL: That sounds
really interesting.

00:14:02.850 --> 00:14:03.740
And I'm curious as well.

00:14:03.740 --> 00:14:05.320
We were talking about
unstructured and structured

00:14:05.320 --> 00:14:06.160
data.

00:14:06.160 --> 00:14:09.220
Are you seeing features
from the unstructured world

00:14:09.220 --> 00:14:12.220
kind of creep their way into
the more structured databases

00:14:12.220 --> 00:14:12.760
as well?

00:14:12.760 --> 00:14:14.177
AMY KRISHNAMOHAN:
Yes, definitely.

00:14:14.177 --> 00:14:16.540
So I think a lot of
people mention about data

00:14:16.540 --> 00:14:18.285
lake becoming a data swamp.

00:14:18.285 --> 00:14:19.660
MARK MANDEL: What
does that mean?

00:14:19.660 --> 00:14:20.220
Tell us more.

00:14:20.220 --> 00:14:21.432
[LAUGHING]

00:14:21.432 --> 00:14:22.390
AMY KRISHNAMOHAN: Yeah.

00:14:22.390 --> 00:14:24.370
A lot of issues that
I think many customers

00:14:24.370 --> 00:14:27.310
are having is that they felt
the unstructured database is

00:14:27.310 --> 00:14:30.322
really cool because they can
just store the data so easily.

00:14:30.322 --> 00:14:31.780
You don't need to
define the table.

00:14:31.780 --> 00:14:34.100
You don't need to define
the schema or anything.

00:14:34.100 --> 00:14:36.910
But once you try to analyze
it, or [INAUDIBLE] data,

00:14:36.910 --> 00:14:40.060
it's becoming really
a big headache.

00:14:40.060 --> 00:14:42.850
Either you need to
create a custom code

00:14:42.850 --> 00:14:45.310
to decipher all
this data, and then

00:14:45.310 --> 00:14:47.840
make sure data is
in the right order.

00:14:47.840 --> 00:14:50.340
So a lot of the
unstructured databases

00:14:50.340 --> 00:14:53.800
now are trying to implement
the SQL layer on the top

00:14:53.800 --> 00:14:57.850
so that people can query
those data easily using

00:14:57.850 --> 00:14:59.920
known languages, like SQL.

00:14:59.920 --> 00:15:02.470
But I think the
most important thing

00:15:02.470 --> 00:15:05.155
is, the reason why I really
like relational databases

00:15:05.155 --> 00:15:09.490
is because it's kind of
putting the disciplines first.

00:15:09.490 --> 00:15:11.590
So you need to have
a clean data first

00:15:11.590 --> 00:15:13.165
if you want to do
a clean analytics.

00:15:13.165 --> 00:15:13.998
[SPRAYING]

00:15:13.998 --> 00:15:16.920
[CLEANER SQUEAKING ON WINDOWS]

00:15:16.920 --> 00:15:17.420
Yeah.

00:15:17.420 --> 00:15:18.337
GABBY FERRARA: So hey.

00:15:18.337 --> 00:15:21.140
You can not have
data consistency

00:15:21.140 --> 00:15:24.167
if you are free to
do whatever you want.

00:15:24.167 --> 00:15:25.250
AMY KRISHNAMOHAN: Exactly.

00:15:25.250 --> 00:15:25.872
Yes.

00:15:25.872 --> 00:15:27.830
GABBY FERRARA: Although,
relational databases--

00:15:27.830 --> 00:15:31.510
and Amy knows probably,
more than I do now--

00:15:31.510 --> 00:15:33.680
you can have unstructured data.

00:15:33.680 --> 00:15:36.890
We just don't recommend.

00:15:36.890 --> 00:15:39.063
Like JSON, or XML even.

00:15:39.063 --> 00:15:39.980
AMY KRISHNAMOHAN: Yes.

00:15:39.980 --> 00:15:40.510
Totally.

00:15:40.510 --> 00:15:41.010
Yeah.

00:15:41.010 --> 00:15:44.270
I think Postgres is actually
one of the best databases

00:15:44.270 --> 00:15:45.290
to handle the JSON.

00:15:45.290 --> 00:15:46.390
That's what I heard.

00:15:46.390 --> 00:15:46.890
Yeah.

00:15:46.890 --> 00:15:50.440
So it is not like structured
databases like Postgres cannot

00:15:50.440 --> 00:15:51.980
handle the unstructured data.

00:15:51.980 --> 00:15:52.550
But yeah.

00:15:52.550 --> 00:15:58.010
Still, in my opinion, still
JSON XML is a bistructured.

00:15:58.010 --> 00:16:01.370
So it does have a structure,
and it is easy to query.

00:16:01.370 --> 00:16:03.980
But when it goes to
really unstructured data,

00:16:03.980 --> 00:16:08.090
a lot of tendency for people
is to, OK, we'll analyze later.

00:16:08.090 --> 00:16:09.680
Let's just store first.

00:16:09.680 --> 00:16:13.820
And I think that is more
about governance or processing

00:16:13.820 --> 00:16:15.500
on how to manage your data.

00:16:15.500 --> 00:16:17.270
MARK MANDEL: I'm not saying
I've stored XML in a database

00:16:17.270 --> 00:16:17.770
before.

00:16:17.770 --> 00:16:19.820
But I've stored XML
in a database before.

00:16:19.820 --> 00:16:21.311
[CREEPY MUSIC]

00:16:21.311 --> 00:16:23.300
[LAUGHING]

00:16:23.300 --> 00:16:25.320
GABBY FERRARA: We all
have our sins, Mark.

00:16:25.320 --> 00:16:26.090
[LAUGHS]

00:16:26.090 --> 00:16:27.298
MARK MANDEL: It was the '90s.

00:16:27.298 --> 00:16:28.670
It was fine.

00:16:28.670 --> 00:16:30.348
In terms of high
availability, one thing

00:16:30.348 --> 00:16:32.390
I don't think we've touched
on is like, how do we

00:16:32.390 --> 00:16:34.960
deal with zones and regions?

00:16:34.960 --> 00:16:35.990
How do we handle that?

00:16:35.990 --> 00:16:38.360
Is that something that's also
managed inside Cloud SQL?

00:16:38.360 --> 00:16:39.777
AMY KRISHNAMOHAN:
At Cloud SQL, we

00:16:39.777 --> 00:16:41.400
provide a zone of
high availability.

00:16:41.400 --> 00:16:44.750
So if that one zone fails,
the other will come.

00:16:44.750 --> 00:16:47.810
If you are looking for a
more regional or global

00:16:47.810 --> 00:16:50.240
high availability, I
really highly recommend

00:16:50.240 --> 00:16:52.240
to look into Cloud Spanner.

00:16:52.240 --> 00:16:55.320
So in relational databases,
actually the higher

00:16:55.320 --> 00:16:59.570
availability and the consistency
is actually a very difficult

00:16:59.570 --> 00:17:01.290
problem to solve.

00:17:01.290 --> 00:17:04.550
So when you have data in,
let's say, the United States,

00:17:04.550 --> 00:17:08.000
and you want to make sure
that the exactly same data is

00:17:08.000 --> 00:17:12.770
available in Japan, so we have
to have a consistency of data.

00:17:12.770 --> 00:17:16.290
Data has to be replicated
exactly the same way.

00:17:16.290 --> 00:17:16.790
Right?

00:17:16.790 --> 00:17:20.950
And then the data gets committed
from the multiple places.

00:17:20.950 --> 00:17:23.800
Like somebody might store
the data from Japan.

00:17:23.800 --> 00:17:27.290
Or somebody might try to insert
data from the United States.

00:17:27.290 --> 00:17:31.050
And all those transactions
happen all at the same time.

00:17:31.050 --> 00:17:34.580
So in order to make sure that
those data are replicated

00:17:34.580 --> 00:17:38.270
consistently, Google really
had made some innovations,

00:17:38.270 --> 00:17:40.580
like something like
a true clock, where

00:17:40.580 --> 00:17:44.550
we know the exact order
of the transaction.

00:17:44.550 --> 00:17:47.960
And then it is really relying
on GPS satellite clock, not

00:17:47.960 --> 00:17:50.700
the normal clock
that you are using.

00:17:50.700 --> 00:17:53.870
So that consistency
sounds really simple.

00:17:53.870 --> 00:17:56.150
But the making
the reality happen

00:17:56.150 --> 00:17:58.430
in the relational database
is actually extremely hard.

00:17:58.430 --> 00:18:01.200
So high availability
around the globe,

00:18:01.200 --> 00:18:04.180
if you want to make that
consistency happen, the Cloud

00:18:04.180 --> 00:18:07.250
Spanner, I think it is the
only database that is available

00:18:07.250 --> 00:18:08.070
right now.

00:18:08.070 --> 00:18:09.445
GABBY FERRARA:
And if people want

00:18:09.445 --> 00:18:11.280
to learn a bit more,
where should they go?

00:18:11.280 --> 00:18:13.280
AMY KRISHNAMOHAN: I would
highly recommend going

00:18:13.280 --> 00:18:15.380
to Cloud.Google.com website.

00:18:15.380 --> 00:18:18.110
It has all the information
about Cloud SQL.

00:18:18.110 --> 00:18:20.840
And then our YouTube has a
lot of interesting information

00:18:20.840 --> 00:18:22.460
about the classical as well.

00:18:22.460 --> 00:18:26.270
So especially if you
search for Cloud Next,

00:18:26.270 --> 00:18:27.980
that has a lot of
interesting sessions

00:18:27.980 --> 00:18:30.175
that we are covering
about the Cloud SQLs too.

00:18:30.175 --> 00:18:31.050
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:18:31.050 --> 00:18:32.717
I'd love to hear more
about your history

00:18:32.717 --> 00:18:34.160
with like the database market.

00:18:34.160 --> 00:18:36.933
I mean, you mentioned previously
there's over 200 databases now.

00:18:36.933 --> 00:18:37.850
AMY KRISHNAMOHAN: Mhm.

00:18:37.850 --> 00:18:39.050
MARK MANDEL: Have
relational databases

00:18:39.050 --> 00:18:41.300
changed along with the
innovation that's happening

00:18:41.300 --> 00:18:42.590
in the database market?

00:18:42.590 --> 00:18:44.030
Or have they stayed
fairly stable?

00:18:44.030 --> 00:18:45.950
Or what's happening
there with customers

00:18:45.950 --> 00:18:48.172
and the technological
changes to databases?

00:18:48.172 --> 00:18:49.880
AMY KRISHNAMOHAN: So
relational databases

00:18:49.880 --> 00:18:52.620
have not been changed a lot.

00:18:52.620 --> 00:18:54.380
Actually, if you
see the real history

00:18:54.380 --> 00:18:58.450
about MySQL or Postgres, gross
or the enterprise databases,

00:18:58.450 --> 00:19:01.140
so SQL Server,
Oracle, [INAUDIBLE],,

00:19:01.140 --> 00:19:04.820
they have a heavy
functionality to make sure

00:19:04.820 --> 00:19:09.200
that the data transaction
is secured and consistent.

00:19:09.200 --> 00:19:11.090
So there's a lot of features.

00:19:11.090 --> 00:19:15.710
But the core of the logic
has not changed a lot.

00:19:15.710 --> 00:19:18.440
The only change that we
have seen in the last

00:19:18.440 --> 00:19:22.220
maybe 5 to 10 years is that
they included functionality

00:19:22.220 --> 00:19:26.610
to handle JSON, XML, like
semi structured data.

00:19:26.610 --> 00:19:29.240
So they have a BLOB column
where they can insert

00:19:29.240 --> 00:19:30.770
those kind of data types.

00:19:30.770 --> 00:19:33.350
I feel that the reason
why it's not changing it

00:19:33.350 --> 00:19:36.890
is because the core requirements
for using relational databases

00:19:36.890 --> 00:19:41.090
is to making sure that the
transaction happens securely.

00:19:41.090 --> 00:19:44.630
And then that doesn't change,
whether in the banking system

00:19:44.630 --> 00:19:46.040
or health care system.

00:19:46.040 --> 00:19:49.730
All the mission critical
systems, that core requirement

00:19:49.730 --> 00:19:50.400
doesn't change.

00:19:50.400 --> 00:19:53.070
So I don't think that
is going to change much.

00:19:53.070 --> 00:19:56.360
GABBY FERRARA: So you told us
what is your favorite Cloud SQL

00:19:56.360 --> 00:19:56.960
feature.

00:19:56.960 --> 00:19:59.352
What is your favorite
database feature?

00:19:59.352 --> 00:20:00.310
AMY KRISHNAMOHAN: Yeah.

00:20:00.310 --> 00:20:03.530
My favorite database
feature is SQL, because it

00:20:03.530 --> 00:20:05.630
is very easy to learn.

00:20:05.630 --> 00:20:08.420
You can make SQL
extremely complicated.

00:20:08.420 --> 00:20:12.420
And I've seen the SQL written
with like 50 lines of code,

00:20:12.420 --> 00:20:13.700
which is actually quite crazy.

00:20:13.700 --> 00:20:14.120
MARK MANDEL: I've done that.

00:20:14.120 --> 00:20:15.537
AMY KRISHNAMOHAN:
But my opinion I

00:20:15.537 --> 00:20:18.360
think is more straight forward,
that you can learn easily.

00:20:18.360 --> 00:20:21.930
And then the power that it has
is actually extremely strong.

00:20:21.930 --> 00:20:24.810
And then the skill set
that brings the people,

00:20:24.810 --> 00:20:26.890
it is so important.

00:20:26.890 --> 00:20:29.280
That's why I think a lot
of unstructured databases

00:20:29.280 --> 00:20:32.557
companies are trying to
employ a SQL layer on the top,

00:20:32.557 --> 00:20:33.640
because they just need it.

00:20:33.640 --> 00:20:34.140
Right?

00:20:34.140 --> 00:20:36.550
If you want to scale
your databases,

00:20:36.550 --> 00:20:38.610
you'd want everyone to use it.

00:20:38.610 --> 00:20:40.440
And if you want
everyone to use it,

00:20:40.440 --> 00:20:42.010
you want to make it easy to use.

00:20:42.010 --> 00:20:43.860
And I think SQL really
makes that happen.

00:20:43.860 --> 00:20:44.670
GABBY FERRARA: I agree.

00:20:44.670 --> 00:20:46.295
MARK MANDEL: So what
sort of fun things

00:20:46.295 --> 00:20:48.570
can people expect from
Cloud SQL in the future?

00:20:48.570 --> 00:20:50.010
AMY KRISHNAMOHAN:
For Cloud SQL we

00:20:50.010 --> 00:20:52.320
just released the
external master feature

00:20:52.320 --> 00:20:55.380
where people can
migrate all from on prem

00:20:55.380 --> 00:20:58.170
to cloud very easily with
the just click of a button.

00:20:58.170 --> 00:21:01.620
So I think we will
try to really provide

00:21:01.620 --> 00:21:03.510
those kind of seamless
experiences more

00:21:03.510 --> 00:21:05.110
to the customer.

00:21:05.110 --> 00:21:07.320
So whether they're
using on prem or cloud,

00:21:07.320 --> 00:21:09.762
it's very easy to
move back and forth.

00:21:09.762 --> 00:21:11.470
MARK MANDEL: So I have
one more question,

00:21:11.470 --> 00:21:14.310
which is, what is the most
interesting or strange thing

00:21:14.310 --> 00:21:18.342
that you've seen someone do
with a relational database?

00:21:18.342 --> 00:21:20.550
AMY KRISHNAMOHAN: The most
interesting that I've seen

00:21:20.550 --> 00:21:22.258
is actually using the
relational database

00:21:22.258 --> 00:21:24.090
as an analytical database.

00:21:24.090 --> 00:21:28.470
There are many companies that
are using MySQL and Postgres

00:21:28.470 --> 00:21:30.840
as their analytical databases.

00:21:30.840 --> 00:21:33.750
And they're complaining
about the performance.

00:21:33.750 --> 00:21:36.680
So My SQL and Postgres is
a transactional database.

00:21:36.680 --> 00:21:38.670
It needs to be used
for transactional, not

00:21:38.670 --> 00:21:39.850
